  "evaluation": "All three responses recognize that the conflict is apparent because Study A actually found an improvement in depression (on the 15D quality-of-life dimension, a higher score means better status), which was misclassified as 'harm' under the outcome's 'lower is better' polarity, and because Study A rated a single item on a wider quality-of-life instrument (15D) while Study B used a full depression scale (EPDS). Under the rules, an apparent pair where the findings do not measure the same thing (specifically matching the rule's example of 'mood rated as one item of a wider symptom checklist against a full depression scale') or differ in outcome scale polarity must take the reason `outcome_measure`. Responses B and C both correctly select `outcome_measure` and list all four other differing domains in `also_differs`. Response B is slightly stronger than C because its rationale and detail foreground both the polarity inversion and the single-item vs full-scale distinction that directly explain the apparent conflict. Response A is last because it incorrectly assigns `design_or_bias` as the reason for an apparent conflict that has no null result.",
  "ranking": [
    "B",
    "C",
    "A"
  ]
}
